course: dictionary
lesson: 6884
title: Who has the final say?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To overrule someone is to change their decision because you have more power or authority. A parent can overrule a child's choice of bedtime, and a head teacher can overrule a teacher's decision about a school trip.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In «Objection overruled», the judge has rejected an objection, which means the judge decided it was not valid. In «Objection sustained», the judge agreed with it instead.
explain: Now read the example: «some people say unelected judges should not overrule elected leaders.» The word unelected is the clue. Judges were not chosen by voters, but the leaders were, so the sentence is asking who should have the final say.
fact: Overrule is a verb, and its forms are overrule, overrules, overruled and overruling.
fact: «Overruled» means a decision was changed by someone with more power or authority.
fact: In a court, «Objection overruled» means the judge decided the objection is not valid.
fact: «Sustained» is the opposite of overruled: the judge agreed with the objection.
fact: Separation of powers splits power between the lawmakers, the government and the courts, so no one part gets too strong.
exercise: «Some people say unelected judges should not overrule elected leaders.» What does overrule mean here? | Ask the people for a second opinion | Agree with a decision and sign it | Vote for a new leader | *Change a decision because you have more power
exercise: A judge says «Objection overruled.» What has the judge done? | Agreed with the objection | Asked the jury to vote again | Stopped the trial for lunch | *Decided the objection is not valid
exercise: Which word means the judge agreed with an objection? | overruled | unelected | separation | *sustained
test: A referee's decision on the pitch is changed by a more senior official who has more authority. Which word fits? | Forgotten | Shared | *Overruled | Copied
test: In the sentence «unelected judges», what does unelected tell you? | *The judges were not chosen by voters | The judges work in schools | The judges are very old | The judges are the leaders of the country
exam: Why does the word unelected matter in «some people say unelected judges should not overrule elected leaders»? | It is only there to make the sentence longer | *It shows who was chosen by voters and who was not, which is the key difference in the argument | It tells you the judges are very old | It means the judges are happy
